#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# composition

#% text
# `composition(f, g, ...)` is the function that applies the functions right to left: `composition(f, g)(x)` is `f(g(x))`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# Applied to several arguments, the last function receives them all: `composition(f, g)(x, y)` is `f(g(x, y))`.

#% item
# On its own a composition stays as it is; `composition()` is the identity.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
composition(f, g)(x)
#% output
#= f(g(x))

#% cell
composition(sqrt, abs)(-16)
#% output
#= 4

#% subsection
# Scope

#% text
# Several arguments for the innermost function:

#% cell
composition(f, g)(x, y)
#% output
#= f(g(x, y))

#% subsection
# Properties and relations

#% text
# `right_composition` applies the functions left to right:

#% cell
right_composition(f, g)(x)
#% output
#= g(f(x))

#% section
# See also

#% text
# `right_composition`, `map`, `nest`, `through`
